insert into public.hr_challenge_answers
  (challenge_code,jurisdiction,answer_text,source_refs,escalation_guidance,is_escalate_default,status,reviewed_by) values
  ('PF26','federal','Since 1 January 2025, intentionally underpaying wages or entitlements is a criminal offence under the Fair Work Act, on top of the existing civil penalties. Honest mistakes are not criminalised, but you still have to fix them. If you find a shortfall: work out exactly who was affected, for what, and how much; back-pay promptly; keep clear records of the correction; and look at the Fair Work Ombudsman''s Voluntary Small Business Wage Compliance Code - following it can protect a small business from criminal referral for that conduct.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - I think I''ve underpaid my employee","Fair Work Ombudsman - Voluntary Small Business Wage Compliance Code","Fair Work Act 2009 (wage theft, from 1 Jan 2025)"}','Systemic, long-running, or large underpayments, anything that could look deliberate, or any uncertainty about the criminal threshold - get an employment lawyer or registered agent BEFORE self-reporting or negotiating repayment with staff.',true,'review','worked-example (needs human sign-off)'),
  ('PF27','federal','Finding an underpayment is not the emergency; mishandling it is. Step through it: confirm the correct award, classification and rate for the period; calculate the gap per person including super and any penalties/overtime; back-pay it and pay the super shortfall; write to each affected employee explaining the correction; and keep the workings. Do not quietly adjust future pays to ''make it back'' - that is not a lawful correction.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - I think I''ve underpaid my employee","ATO - Super guarantee charge / shortfalls"}','Large, multi-year, or multi-employee shortfalls, or anything that might be viewed as deliberate, cross into wage-theft territory - take advice before acting, and see PF26.',true,'review','worked-example (needs human sign-off)'),
  ('PM9','federal','You cannot take ''adverse action'' (dismiss, demote, cut hours, or refuse to hire) against someone because they have or exercised a workplace right - making a complaint or enquiry about their employment, taking leave, or union involvement - or because of a protected attribute. General protections have no minimum employment period and no small-business exemption, and if a claim is lodged the reverse onus applies: you must prove the action was not for a prohibited reason. Timing is the trap - acting soon after someone raised a concern or took leave invites a claim even when your reason was genuine.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Protection from discrimination at work","Fair Work Act 2009 ss340-361"}','Any dismissal or discipline close in time to a complaint, leave, illness, injury, or pregnancy - get legal advice before you act. This is the single highest reverse-onus risk in the People stream.',true,'review','worked-example (needs human sign-off)'),
  ('PM10','federal','A redundancy is only ''genuine'' if the job itself (not the person) is no longer needed because of operational change, you met any consultation obligation in the award or agreement, and it was not reasonable to redeploy the person elsewhere. Businesses with fewer than 15 employees are exempt from statutory redundancy PAY, but not from notice, and not from unfair-dismissal or general-protections exposure if the redundancy is not genuine. Replacing the person soon after, or using ''redundancy'' to move on someone you simply do not rate, defeats the genuineness test.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Redundancy","Fair Work Act 2009 s119 (redundancy pay), s121 (small business exemption), s389 (genuine redundancy)"}','Selecting between people for one role, larger restructures, or any redundancy that could be read as a disguised dismissal - take advice before notifying anyone.',true,'review','worked-example (needs human sign-off)'),
  ('PM7','federal','Summary (instant) dismissal is only lawful for genuine serious misconduct - such as theft, fraud, serious safety breaches, or violence - and even then you must run a fair process: put the specific allegations to the employee, give them a real chance to respond, and decide on the evidence rather than the heat of the moment. Getting the ''serious misconduct'' call wrong, or skipping the process, is one of the most common ways a small business loses an unfair-dismissal claim.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Dismissal","Fair Work Regulations 2009 reg 1.07 (meaning of serious misconduct)"}','Contested facts, a senior employee, or any case where you are not certain the conduct clears the serious-misconduct bar - get advice before you dismiss on the spot.',true,'review','worked-example (needs human sign-off)'),
  ('RA17','federal','Sponsoring an overseas worker means becoming an approved business sponsor and meeting employer obligations under the migration system - nominating the role, paying at least the market salary rate, keeping records, and honouring ongoing sponsor obligations. This is a Department of Home Affairs process, separate from Fair Work. It can be worth it for genuinely hard-to-fill roles, but it carries real cost, lead time, and ongoing compliance load, so weigh it against redesigning or re-scoping the role.','{"Department of Home Affairs - Employing and sponsoring someone","Home Affairs - Skills in Demand / employer sponsorship obligations"}','Sponsorship is specialist - use a registered migration agent or immigration lawyer. Do not self-navigate nomination, salary-rate, or sponsor-obligation compliance.',true,'review','worked-example (needs human sign-off)'),
  ('RS22','federal','If you use AI to screen, rank, or shortlist candidates, you remain legally responsible for the outcome - including bias you did not intend, and bias that originates in a vendor''s algorithm through proxies like postcode, name, or gaps in work history. Australian anti-discrimination law and Fair Work general protections apply in full; there is no AI carve-out. From 10 December 2026, Privacy Act transparency rules require your privacy policy to disclose the personal information used in automated decisions such as automated screening or interview-invite calls.','{"Australian Human Rights Commission - guidance on AI and recruitment","OAIC - Privacy Act automated decision-making transparency (from 10 Dec 2026)"}','Before deploying any automated screening tool, and on any bias or privacy complaint - get legal/privacy advice and run vendor due diligence on how the tool was tested for bias.',true,'review','worked-example (needs human sign-off)'),
  ('PF8','federal','Start with what your business actually does, then read the coverage clause (usually clause 4) of each award that looks close. Match each role to a classification by what the person mainly does, not their job title. If your business spans activities, like a café that also caters, different roles may sit under different awards. The trap is thinking that paying above minimum wage means the award doesn''t matter. Penalty rates, allowances and other award clauses still apply unless a set-off is properly structured, and no one can contract out of an award. Genuinely award-free roles are rare, mostly high-income or managerial. If an enterprise agreement applies, it replaces the award for those staff. Re-check coverage whenever the business changes what it does. A wrong guess is underpayment, whatever your intent.','{"Fair Work Commission - Which award applies","Fair Work Ombudsman - About awards","Fair Work Act 2009 Part 2-3"}','If your business spans more than one industry, a role doesn''t clearly match any classification, or you are treating someone as award-free, get expert help before you set pay. A wrong coverage call creates underpayment exposure that applies regardless of intent and builds with every pay run.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF9','federal','Pull out the award and read the classification descriptors against what the person actually does day to day, not their job title or what you hired them for. Classification is judged by the principal purpose of the role, so a ''junior'' who now runs the store may sit at a higher level. Check this at hiring and again whenever duties change, because roles drift and pay often does not follow. That drift is the trap. Each pay period at the wrong level adds to a back-pay bill, and underpayment is strict liability, so not knowing is no defence. If duties have moved up, reclassify and pay the correct minimum from now, then work out what is owed for the past. Do not quietly fix it going forward and hope. Check the current rate for the level in the Fair Work Pay Calculator or your award.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 s45","JB Solicitors - Fair Work Awards: Your Complete Guide (https://jbsolicitors.com.au/employment-awards/)","Australian Industry Group - Understanding modern award coverage (https://www.australianindustrygroup.com.au/resourcecentre/resource-centre-blogs/hr-blogs/understanding-modern-award-coverage/)"}','If someone has been working above their classification for a while, or you are unsure which level fits, get advice before you reclassify or calculate back pay. Getting the level or the back-pay amount wrong is an underpayment exposure with penalties.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF3','federal','Pull out every contractor arrangement you have and compare the paperwork with how the work actually happens. Under the Fair Work Act, what counts now is the real substance and practical reality of the whole relationship, not just the contract wording. An ABN, invoices and a contractor agreement do not settle it. The trap is drift. Someone engaged as a contractor years ago may now work set hours, use your tools and work only for you. The test applies for as long as the relationship runs, so an arrangement that was fine at the start may not be fine now. The older contract-first test still applies under some other laws, so the answer can differ depending on which law is asking. Getting it wrong can mean unpaid NES entitlements, super guarantee charge, PAYG withholding and possible sham contracting penalties.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Closing Loopholes","Fair Work Act 2009 s15AA","Fair Work Ombudsman - Whole of relationship test"}','If anyone you treat as a contractor works set hours, uses your equipment or works only for you, or if you are thinking of reclassifying them, get advice before you act. Misclassification exposure covers unpaid entitlements, super and tax, and can extend to sham contracting penalties.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF6','federal','Look at how each casual actually works, not what their letter says. A person is only casual if there is no firm advance commitment to continuing and indefinite work, judged on the practical reality of the relationship. The trap is the long-term casual on the same regular, predictable roster, the classic retail and hospitality pattern. That pattern now risks them being treated as permanent, with back-pay of leave entitlements on top of the casual loading you already paid. Length of service alone does not decide it; the commitment does. Update casual contracts and letters to reflect the new definition, check your award''s casual conversion clause, and decide whether to offer permanent work or wait for the employee to use the Employee Choice Pathway. Set-off rules for loading already paid may reduce the exposure, but do not rely on them.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Closing Loopholes","Fair Work Act 2009 Part 2-2 Div 4A","Aitken Partners - Casual employment under the Closing the Loopholes legislation","Fair Work Ombudsman - Casual employees"}','If a long-term casual on a regular roster disputes their status, asks about conversion, or raises leave back-pay, get advice before you respond. A wrong call here can mean back-paying leave entitlements and an underpayment dispute.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF1','federal','Put it in writing, even though the law does not always demand it. For most ongoing staff the Fair Work Act does not require a written contract, but one is effectively needed to properly engage casuals, fixed-term staff and anyone on an individual flexibility arrangement. Whatever you agree, give every new employee the Fair Work Information Statement when they start, as missing it can attract a civil penalty. Spell out their hours, classification and the award that covers them, because a contract silent on these causes disputes later. The trap is the old template from Google. The NES and the modern award sit underneath every contract, and any term below them is void and replaced by the minimum. The void term does not save you: it leaves you exposed to back-pay and penalties. Check the award or the Fair Work Pay Calculator before anyone signs.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - About employment contracts","Fair Work Act 2009 s125"}','If an existing contract or template may pay below the award or NES, or you are unsure which award or classification applies, get advice before acting. A sub-minimum term creates back-pay and penalty exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF13','federal','Treat the National Employment Standards as the minimum every employee gets, whatever your award, agreement or contract says. The list covers maximum weekly hours, flexible work requests, parental leave, various paid and unpaid leave, public holidays, notice and redundancy pay, casual conversion rights, and the Fair Work Information Statement. Check your contracts and award against it. The trap is assuming that if your award says nothing about something, there is no entitlement. Parental leave, notice and redundancy exist in the NES with no award equivalent, so your award may never prompt you. A contract cannot sign the NES away, because any term that gives less has no effect. Casuals do not get every NES right in the same way, so check which apply before you assume. Give every employee the Fair Work Information Statement, because failing to can attract civil penalties.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - National Employment Standards","Fair Work Ombudsman - Introduction to the National Employment Standards fact sheet","Fair Work Commission - National Employment Standards","Fair Work Act 2009 Part 2-2"}','If you find a contract or award term that gives less than the NES, or a staff member has missed out on an NES entitlement such as notice, redundancy pay or parental leave, get advice before fixing it, as this can be an underpayment. Anything involving parental leave or redundancy also needs a human.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF14','federal','Before you direct anyone to take leave or pay it out, check your award, because it sets the rules for both. Annual leave does not expire. Full-time staff build up four weeks a year (five for some shiftworkers). It accrues progressively, and unused leave carries over indefinitely. Part-timers accrue on a pro-rata basis. If someone is sitting on a large balance, your award may let you direct them to take excess leave, but you have to follow the award''s process and notice rules. Cashing out is only allowed where the award permits it, and only with a written agreement and proper records. The trap is the quick informal payout. A cash-out without the right paperwork can be treated as never having happened, so you may still owe the leave you thought you paid. Also check whether leave loading applies under the award or contract.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - National Employment Standards","Fair Work Ombudsman - Paid annual leave & sick & carer''s leave - Frequently Asked Questions","Fair Work Act 2009 ss87-94"}','Get advice before you act if a leave payout has already been made without a written agreement, or if you are unsure whether your award allows you to direct excess leave or cash it out. A wrong move here can leave you still owing leave you thought you had paid, plus a record-keeping breach.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF15','federal','Treat sick leave and carer''s leave as one balance. Full-time and part-time staff build up paid personal/carer''s leave as they work: 10 days for each year of service, pro-rata for part-timers. Whether they are ill or injured, or caring for a family or household member, it comes out of the same bank. Unused leave carries over year to year. Unlike annual leave, it generally is not paid out when they leave. Casuals do not get the paid version, but they can take 2 days'' unpaid carer''s leave per occasion. You can ask for evidence such as a medical certificate or statutory declaration, but the request has to be reasonable. The trap is demanding a certificate for every single-day absence without checking your award and policy. Refusing valid leave or asking for unreasonable evidence can breach the NES.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Sick and carer''s leave","Fair Work Ombudsman - Paid sick and carer''s leave","Fair Work Commission - NES Personal and Carer''s Leave fact sheet","Fair Work Act 2009 ss96-107"}','Get advice before you refuse leave, question a pattern of absences, or discipline anyone over sick or carer''s leave. Getting it wrong can breach the NES or look like adverse action against someone who is ill or caring for family.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF10','federal','Before your first pay run after 1 July, check every employee''s rate against the new minimum for their award and classification. The Fair Work Commission''s Annual Wage Review lifts award minimums from 1 July, and awards can also change mid-year. Use the Fair Work Pay Calculator or your award, not last year''s rate card. The trap is assuming someone paid above award last year is still above award now. Recent increases have been large, so that margin may have shrunk or gone. Also check casual loading, increments, and any piece rates or allowances that move with the base rate. Timing matters: the new rate applies from the effective date, so even a short lag is an underpayment. If you find one, fix it and back-pay to that date.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - About awards","Fair Work Ombudsman - Pay and Conditions Tool (Pay Calculator)"}','If you find you have paid below the current award rate, or you plan to absorb a wage rise into an existing above-award payment, get advice before you act. Getting either wrong creates underpayment exposure that runs from the effective date.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF11','federal','Pull up the award that covers each person''s actual work and read its penalty, overtime and allowance clauses before you finalise the roster, not after payday. Most awards add extra payments on top of the base rate for weekend, public holiday, evening and night work, for overtime, and for allowances such as uniform, tool, travel, meal or first aid. Each one has its own trigger. Overtime is the trap. Depending on the award it can start under a daily, weekly or rostered-hours test, so a simple weekly hours cut-off is not a safe rule. Some allowances are owed regardless of hours worked, or because of a specific duty like handling cash. Check amounts with the Fair Work Pay Calculator rather than from memory. Missing these is the biggest single driver of underpayments, and it counts even when it was an honest mistake.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Pay and Conditions Tool (Pay Calculator)","Fair Work Act 2009 s45","JB Solicitors - Fair Work Awards (https://jbsolicitors.com.au/employment-awards/)"}','If you find you have missed penalty rates, overtime or allowances for past shifts, get advice before you fix it. Back-pay calculation and how you handle it both carry underpayment exposure. Also get advice if an enterprise agreement may apply or you are unsure which award covers the work.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF21','federal','Pick one system for pay, hours, leave and super records, and keep everything for seven years, including records for people who have left. The format matters less than being complete and consistent. A mix of spreadsheets, texted rosters and paper timesheets often misses required content. Records must be in English, legible, accurate and easy to produce on request. When you fix an error, note the correction clearly rather than overwriting it. Common gaps are leave balances, super contribution details, and the history of each person''s classification and pay rate. The trap is thinking poor records only risk a fine. If someone claims they were underpaid and your records are inadequate, the burden shifts to you to prove you paid correctly. Missing records can lose a wage dispute on their own. If you find gaps, especially for ex-staff, get advice before trying to fill them in after the fact.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Record-keeping and pay slips fact sheet","Fair Work Act 2009 ss535-536","Fair Work Regulations 2009 regs 3.31-3.46"}','Get advice if you find missing or incomplete records, receive a Fair Work Ombudsman request, or face any underpayment claim. Poor records shift the burden of proof onto you, and reconstructing records after the fact can make things worse.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF22','federal','Pull up your last pay slip and check it against what the rules require. It must go out within one working day of pay day and show your business name and ABN, the employee''s name, the pay period, the payment date, and the gross and net amounts. Any bonus, loading, allowance, penalty rate or overtime must appear as its own line. Where you made a super contribution, show the fund and account details. The trap is the lump sum. Showing one gross pay figure is a breach even if every dollar paid is correct. Basic payroll software usually gets the format right, but manual payroll or software not built for Australian awards often does not. Civil penalties apply per breach, so one gap repeated every pay run for every employee adds up fast.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Record-keeping and pay slips fact sheet","Fair Work Ombudsman - Pay slips","Fair Work Ombudsman - Record-keeping","Fair Work Act 2009 s536","Fair Work Regulations 2009 reg 3.46"}','If checking your pay slips shows that allowances, penalty rates or overtime were not actually paid, treat it as a suspected underpayment and get advice before you fix it. The same applies if the Fair Work Ombudsman has contacted you about your records.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('PF24','federal','Work out super on each person''s ordinary time earnings (OTE), not their gross pay, and pay it to the right fund on time every pay cycle. Casuals get super too, the same as any other eligible employee. The super guarantee rate has reached its final legislated level, with no further increases scheduled, so check the current figure with the ATO rather than relying on memory. OTE is the trap. Overtime is usually left out, but many allowances count, and bonuses and leave loading each have their own rules. Applying super to gross pay can leave you over or under, depending on the pay mix. Before you put a new starter into your default fund, do the ATO stapled fund check. A shortfall is not fixed by quietly paying late. It triggers the Superannuation Guarantee Charge, which costs much more than the missed amount.','{"Superannuation Guarantee (Administration) Act 1992 (Cth)"}','If you find super has been calculated on the wrong base or paid late or short, get advice from your accountant or the ATO before you correct it. A shortfall is an underpayment and brings Superannuation Guarantee Charge exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF25','federal','Check now whether your payroll software or provider supports Payday Super, and plan your cash flow as if super leaves your account with every pay run. Under Payday Super, you pay super at the same time as wages, within a short window after each payday, instead of quarterly. The quarterly gap many small businesses quietly relied on for cash flow goes away. The trap is treating this as a timing tweak. The calculation base also changes to ''Qualifying Earnings'', which is not exactly the same as the ordinary time earnings base you use now, and the maximum contribution base moves from quarterly to annual. Late or short payments trigger a faster superannuation guarantee charge. The charge itself becomes tax-deductible, but penalties and interest do not. Confirm the start date and exact payment window with the ATO.','{"ATO - Super guarantee penalties (Super for employers > Quarterly super to 30 June 2026 > Missed and late super guarantee payments)"}','If you have paid super late or short, or are unsure what counts as Qualifying Earnings for your staff, get advice from the ATO or a registered tax or BAS agent before the next pay run. Unpaid super is an underpayment, and under the new regime the charge triggers faster.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM17','federal','Start by finding out why people leave and why they stay, before you assume it is pay. Workload is the reason employers most often give for people leaving, and in a small team one manager''s habits can drive most of your turnover. You do not need survey software. Hold a short stay conversation with each person every few months, ask the same few questions, and have a proper exit conversation when someone resigns, so "it''s not working out" turns into something you can act on. Keep a simple record of who left, when and why. Then spend where it counts: learning and development, flexible work and wellbeing support are the levers employers use most. The trap is reading lower turnover as good news. Engagement is weak, and people may be staying because they are worried, not because they are happy.','{"AHRI - Quarterly Australian Work Outlook (Q1 2025 report)","AHRI - Quarterly Australian Work Outlook (Q3 2025 report)"}','If a stay or exit conversation raises bullying, harassment, discrimination, unpaid hours or underpayment, or a workload complaint affecting someone''s health, stop treating it as a retention issue and get advice before you respond. Those are complaints with legal exposure, and mishandling them can turn a resignation into a claim.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('PM20','federal','Treat this as applying to you. The positive duty under the Sex Discrimination Act covers every employer, with no small-business exemption. You must take reasonable and proportionate steps to prevent sexual harassment, sex-based harassment, sex discrimination and related victimisation before anything happens, not just respond after a complaint. For a small team, proportionate steps include a short written policy, a clear way for staff to raise concerns, covering expectations at onboarding, a periodic refresher, and a prompt process for handling any complaint. The standard flexes with your size; the duty does not. The trap is a policy nobody has seen. Keep records showing it was communicated, trained on and enforced, because that is your evidence of compliance. ''We''ve never had a complaint'' does not show compliance. The Australian Human Rights Commission can inquire without a complaint and has flagged retail and hospitality as priority industries.','{"Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth) s47C","Australian Human Rights Commission - What are your obligations? (humanrights.gov.au/know-your-rights/obligations-as-a-business-and-organisation/what-are-your-obligations)","Fair Work Ombudsman - Rules about workplace sexual harassment"}','Any complaint or report of harassment or discrimination, or any contact from the Australian Human Rights Commission, needs professional advice before you respond. A mishandled response can create victimisation and enforcement exposure on top of the original issue.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM6','federal','Before you warn anyone, write down the specific allegation, check the facts, and give them written notice of a meeting with time to prepare (commonly 24 to 48 hours) and the option to bring a support person. Do not refuse one. In the meeting, genuinely listen to their side before you decide anything. Then record the outcome and when any warning will be reviewed. Standard practice is graduated: verbal warning, written warning, final written warning, then termination, scaled to how serious the issue is. Jumping to a final warning for a first minor issue is a common mistake. So is doing nothing until it blows up. Treat every staff member the same way, because inconsistency can look like discrimination. The trap: a poor process can make a dismissal unfair even when the conduct really happened. Under 15 staff? Follow the Small Business Fair Dismissal Code checklist.','{"Fair Work Commission - Dismissal rules for small business owners","Fair Work Act 2009 s387","Small Business Fair Dismissal Code"}','Any discipline where dismissal is a possible outcome, where you are the person who was wronged, or where it lands close in time to a complaint, leave, illness, injury or pregnancy - get advice before you act. A rushed or inconsistent process can turn real misconduct into an unfair dismissal or adverse action claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM12','federal','Before you say no, talk it through with them. The Fair Work Act requires you to discuss the request and genuinely try to reach agreement on an alternative before you refuse. Reply in writing within 21 days. Eligible employees include parents, carers, people over 55, people with a disability and people experiencing family and domestic violence. You can only refuse on reasonable business grounds specific to the role and your business, such as cost, capacity, practicality, efficiency or customer-service impact. ''We''re too small'' is the trap: business size is one factor to weigh, not an automatic reason to refuse. If you refuse, write down the grounds and the alternatives you considered. A verbal no with no paper trail can now go to the Fair Work Commission, which can order you to grant the request.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Requests for flexible working arrangements fact sheet","Fair Work Act 2009 ss65-65A"}','If the request comes from someone who is pregnant, returning from parental leave, has a disability or illness, or is experiencing family and domestic violence, or if they have lodged a dispute with the Fair Work Commission, get advice before refusing. A poorly handled refusal can be overturned by the Commission and may be linked to other claims.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM1','federal','Treat probation as a check-in point in the contract, not a free pass to dismiss. Probation has no standing under the Fair Work Act. The legal clock that matters is the minimum employment period: 6 months, or 12 months if you are a small business with fewer than 15 employees. Until that period passes, the person generally cannot make an unfair dismissal claim, and that is all it changes. They still get full leave and other NES entitlements from day one. General protections and discrimination claims have no waiting period at all. Timing is the trap. Letting someone go soon after they raise a complaint, a safety issue or a leave request can look like adverse action, probation or not. So always have a documented reason and a fair conversation. If you are unsure about someone, extend probation in writing before it lapses.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Probation","Fair Work Ombudsman - Before starting employment","Fair Work Act 2009 Part 3-1 (general protections)"}','Any dismissal during probation or early tenure, especially soon after a complaint, safety issue, leave request, illness or pregnancy, needs advice before you act. General protections claims apply from day one with uncapped compensation.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM4','federal','Start writing things down now. Each time you raise an issue, make a short dated file note of what you said, the specific standard they are missing, and their response. Mentioning it a few times with no note is the trap - it is hard to show a warning happened if nothing was written. Vague feedback like ''pick up your game'' cannot be tied to a measurable standard later, so name the actual gap. Act at the first pattern, not when you are furious. Documenting is not a paper trail against you; having no trail is the risk. Under the unfair dismissal test in the Fair Work Act and the Small Business Fair Dismissal Code, the employer must show the person was warned and given a chance to improve before a performance-based dismissal. Without that, a dismissal can be found unfair even when the concern was genuine.','{"Fair Work Commission - Unfair dismissals benchbook - Warnings","Fair Work Act 2009 s387","Small Business Fair Dismissal Code"}','If you are thinking about a formal warning, a PIP or dismissal, or are unsure whether the issue is conduct or capacity, get advice before you act. Moving to dismissal with no documented warning and no chance to improve is the most common way these dismissals are found unfair.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM5','federal','Before you start a PIP, be honest about whether you have already decided to let them go. If you have, the PIP is a trap for you, not them. A PIP is not a legal requirement, but if you use one and then dismiss, the Fair Work Commission looks hard at the process, not just the outcome. Make sure the problem is capacity, not misconduct. Set specific, measurable targets they can realistically hit, and a reasonable timeframe for the role. Too short is a common mistake. Name the support you will give, schedule regular check-ins and write down what was said at each one. Tell them plainly what happens if targets are not met. Decide the outcome at the review date, not before. A dismissal after a PIP that was vague, rushed or unsupported can be found unfair even if performance really was poor.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 s387","Coleman Greig Lawyers - Performance Improvement Plans in Australia: Legal Guide (https://colemangreig.com.au/insights/publications/employee-performance-improvement-plan-overview/)","Parke Lawyers - Managing Underperformance and Performance Improvement Plans (https://pl.com.au/information-centre/managing-underperformance-performance-improvement-plans-australia)"}','Get advice before you start any PIP that could end in dismissal, and before deciding the outcome, especially if the person has raised a complaint, taken leave, is ill or injured, or is pregnant. A flawed PIP process can make a dismissal unfair even when performance was genuinely poor.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM3','federal','Sort your policies by legal weight before buying any template pack. No law requires an employee handbook, but several obligations effectively force written policies: WHS systems for hazard reporting, consultation and incidents; an anti-harassment policy, your key evidence of ''reasonable steps'' under the Sex Discrimination Act positive duty, which has no small-business exemption; a privacy policy once privacy law applies to you; and a grievance process. Then document leave, rostering and pay processes so they match the NES and your award. WHS law varies by state, and Victoria has its own. The trap is reading ''no handbook needed'' as ''no policies needed''. Other traps: a years-old template never updated for the right to disconnect, psychosocial hazards or the positive duty, and no record that staff received and acknowledged it. Check policies against the award and contracts in force, and review them when the law changes.','{"Australian Human Rights Commission - What are your obligations?","Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth) s47C","Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth) Part 2-2 (National Employment Standards)","Privacy Act 1988 (Cth)","Safe Work Australia - Model Work Health and Safety Act"}','If you have received a harassment, discrimination or bullying complaint, or an inquiry from the AHRC or a WHS regulator, get advice before relying on or rewriting policies, because missing or outdated policies can be used as evidence you did not take reasonable steps.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM8','federal','Before you dismiss anyone, check your headcount, how long they have worked for you, and whether you can show the Small Business Fair Dismissal Code steps on paper. Count regular casuals and staff in associated entities to confirm you are under 15 employees. In a small business, the minimum period before they can claim is 12 months. Following the Code is a complete defence, but it is not a free pass. You still need a valid reason, and for performance issues they must have had a prior warning. Write each step down as it happens, because records are what win at conciliation. The trap is thinking being small makes you safe. Claims are cheap to lodge, so even weak ones cost you time. If one arrives, decide whether to contest or settle, then fix the process that caused it.','{"Fair Work Commission - Dismissal rules for small business owners","Fair Work Act 2009 Part 3-2 (unfair dismissal)","Fair Work Act 2009 s388 (Small Business Fair Dismissal Code)"}','Any dismissal, or a conciliation notice or claim once lodged, needs legal advice before you act or respond. Get advice especially if you are unsure about your headcount, the person''s length of service, or whether your Code steps are documented, because a wrong move can lead to reinstatement or compensation orders.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM14','federal','Treat psychosocial hazards like a trip hazard: identify them, assess them, control them and review them. Under WHS law this is a core duty, not a wellbeing extra, and small businesses are not exempt. Hazards include high workload, low job control, unclear roles, poor support, isolated work, aggressive customers, and bullying or harassment. Ask your staff what affects them, because consultation is part of the duty. Then fix the causes, for example roster changes, a clear reporting channel, training or a workload review. Write down what you found and did, and review it regularly and after any incident or complaint. The trap is handling problems as they come up, or treating stress as a personal issue for an EAP. The detail depends on your state or territory WHS law, and Victoria has separate but similar rules.','{"Safe Work Australia - Model Code of Practice: Managing psychosocial hazards at work","Safe Work Australia - Psychosocial hazards","Work Health and Safety (Managing Psychosocial Hazards at Work) Code of Practice 2024 (Cth), Federal Register of Legislation F2024L01380"}','Any bullying, harassment or aggression complaint, any incident involving psychological injury, or any contact from a WHS regulator - get specialist WHS or legal advice before you act, because mishandling it can lead to improvement notices, investigation or prosecution.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM21','federal','If you think someone is burnt out, have a supportive conversation before any performance one. Ask what is going on, what their workload looks like and what would help. The trap is reading disengagement, rising absence or slipping quality as an attitude problem and starting a performance improvement plan, when the cause may be the job itself. Burnout is not just a personal resilience issue. Excessive workload, poor support and low control are named psychosocial hazards, so this sits under your work health and safety duty. WHS rules come from your state or territory, not one national law. Having no EAP budget does not mean you have no options. Look at a workload review, flexible arrangements, low-cost EAP alternatives and free government or industry resources. Around half of Australian workers report burnout in the past year, so treat it as common, not rare.','{"Beyond Blue - 1 in 2 Australians Facing Workplace Burnout (media release, June 2025) https://www.beyondblue.org.au/about/media/media-releases/1-in-2-Australians-Facing-Workplace-Burnout","Corporate Mental Health Alliance Australia - Leading Mentally Healthy Workplaces Survey Report 2025 https://cmhaa.org.au/wp-content/uploads/CMHAA-Survey-Report-2025_digital_Final.pdf"}','If burnout shows up as extended absence, a complaint, a WHS incident, or you are thinking about discipline or a performance plan, get advice before you act. A wrong step here mishandles a psychosocial hazard and an illness issue at the same time, and that creates WHS regulator exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA6','federal','Write the ad so the wrong people rule themselves out. Keep it short and use a simple structure: a two-line role summary, what a normal day actually looks like, the must-haves (only the genuine ones), what you offer, and the salary. Leaving the salary out tends to cut both the number and the quality of applicants. Add a few screening questions that test the must-haves so you are not reading every CV. Drop jargon and generic template phrases. A plain, honest picture of a small business helps the right people see themselves in the role. The trap is skipping the final read-through. Check the ad describes the real job and that a job actually exists, because misleading ads can breach the Australian Consumer Law. Check the wording for anything discriminatory, and check the advertised pay meets the award for the work.','{"ACCC - Misleading job and business opportunity ads","Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (Cth) Sch 2 (Australian Consumer Law) s31"}','If the ad sets requirements tied to age, sex, family status, disability or similar, or advertises pay you have not checked against the award, get advice before posting. Discriminatory wording or an under-award advertised rate creates exposure that cannot be fixed after applicants respond.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA7','federal','Before you publish, check each requirement: does the job genuinely need it, or is it standing in for age, sex, race, disability, pregnancy, family or carer responsibilities, or another protected attribute? Describe what the person must be able to do, not who they are. "Young and energetic" and "recent graduate only" point at age. Instead of "native English speaker", state the level of English the work actually needs. The trap is the disguised version. An experience threshold the role does not need, a physical requirement the job does not involve, or "cultural fit" used to screen people out can be indirect discrimination even when nothing says it outright. Do not ask about kids, pregnancy or health in the ad or early screening. Federal and state or territory laws apply side by side, and complaints can go to the Australian Human Rights Commission and on to court.','{"Attorney-General''s Department - Australia''s anti-discrimination law","Fair Work Commission - Discrimination","Age Discrimination Act 2004 (Cth)","Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth)","Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (Cth)","Racial Discrimination Act 1975 (Cth)"}','If a rejected applicant complains, mentions a protected attribute, or lodges a complaint with the Australian Human Rights Commission or a state body, get legal advice before you respond. If you believe a requirement linked to age, sex, disability or similar is genuinely needed for the role, also get advice before you advertise, because a wrong call here is a discrimination claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA11','federal','Start by finding the right award and classification for the work the person will actually do, then check the minimum in the Fair Work Pay Calculator. That is your floor, not your answer. Next, look at the market: free recruiter salary guides and current job ads for similar roles in your area. Use more than one source, because the guides are produced by recruitment agencies and can run high, and one competitor ad is an anecdote, not a benchmark. Allow for location, since regional and city rates can differ. Then set a band by experience level, leaving room to negotiate without paying a new starter more than people already doing the same job. The trap is classification. Get it wrong and no advertised salary protects you, because paying below the award or the National Minimum Wage is unlawful whatever was agreed.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Pay and Conditions Tool (Pay Calculator)","Fair Work Ombudsman - Find my award"}','If you are unsure which award or classification covers the role, or you suspect current staff in similar roles are paid below the award, get advice before you set the band. A wrong classification creates underpayment exposure that grows with every pay run.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA12','federal','You do not have to put the salary in the ad. There is no general legal requirement in Australia to disclose it. But leaving it out now has a cost: ads with a visible salary draw noticeably more applications, and many candidates read "competitive salary" as a red flag. If you publish, pick a fixed figure or a range. Keep any range tight enough to show you know what the role is worth, and match it to your approved budget, or expect friction at offer stage. Decide whether you are quoting base only or the full package with super and any bonus, and say which. Two traps: the ad must not misrepresent the pay, and any figure you print must not sit below the award or minimum for that role. Check the award before you post. Staff may notice the number, so know where your current pay sits.','{"SEEK Employer - Why it''s important to include salary in your job ad - https://au.employer.seek.com/hiring-advice/article/why-its-important-to-include-salary-in-your-job-ad","SBS News - Australian job ads mostly not disclosing salaries, SEEK finds - https://www.sbs.com.au/news/article/the-key-pay-detail-job-seekers-want-that-employers-arent-giving-them/vpykd33qk"}','If you are unsure which award covers the role or whether your advertised figure meets its minimum, check with the Fair Work Ombudsman or an adviser before posting. Advertising or offering below the minimum creates underpayment exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA13','federal','Before you post any job ad, check which award covers the role and the classification the person will actually work at. Then confirm the current minimum rate with the Fair Work Pay Calculator. Advertising a rate below the award, enterprise agreement or national minimum wage is a specific offence in its own right. Fair Work Inspectors actively search job sites and issue fines, and enforcement is increasing. The trap is the rate you have always paid. Award rates go up each year after the annual wage review, so an ad that was compliant last year can be illegal now. If you advertise a flat rate, it must genuinely cover every penalty and loading the person would otherwise get, and you should be able to show how. Re-check your ads and templates whenever rates change.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Job ads (https://www.fairwork.gov.au/starting-employment/job-ads)","Fair Work Ombudsman media release - Fair Work Ombudsman crackdown on dodgy job ads, June 2024","Fair Work Ombudsman media release - Employers warned over dodgy job ads after doubling of fines issued, October 2025"}','If you are unsure which award or classification covers the role, or you find you have been advertising or paying below the minimum, get advice before you hire or adjust pay. A non-compliant ad can mean a fine, and the same mistake in actual pay is underpayment exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA3','federal','Start with outcomes, not tasks. Write down the three to five things this person must deliver for the business, then list the duties that produce them. Split skills into must-have and nice-to-have, and decide who they report to and at what level. Do not just reuse your old ad or an online template. The trap is describing what the last person did rather than what the role needs now. Add a simple success measure for each outcome, because you cannot later manage performance against duties that were never accurately written down. Before you advertise, check the duties against the right award classification, because an inaccurate description is where classification errors and underpayment start. Finally, decide whether this document also becomes the contract schedule or stays a separate recruitment document.','{"Michael Page AU - 5 common hiring pain points every hiring manager in Australia can relate to (https://www.michaelpage.com.au/recruitment-expertise/employer-insights/5-common-hiring-pain-points-every-hiring-manager-australia-can)","Development at Work Australia - 41% of Employers Experienced Pain Points in their Recruitment Efforts this Quarter (https://www.developmentatwork.com.au/2026/02/20/recruitment-pain-points/)"}','If you are unsure which award or classification the duties fall under, or the role is being rewritten for an existing employee, get advice before you advertise or change anything. A wrong classification creates underpayment exposure that backpay alone may not fix.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA2','federal','Decide the engagement type from how the work will actually be done, not from what is cheapest or simplest. If you will set their hours, supervise them, supply the tools, and they will work only for you, the law is likely to treat them as an employee whatever the contract says and whether or not they have an ABN. The trap is asking for an ABN as a condition of the job, or ''trialling'' someone as a contractor. That can be sham contracting, which is unlawful under the Fair Work Act. Getting it wrong can mean back-paying leave and super, civil penalties for each breach, and tax and super guarantee exposure with the ATO. Casual is not a free option either, because casual conversion rights still apply. Write down why you chose the engagement type, and look at it again if the arrangement starts to change.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Sham contracting (https://www.fairwork.gov.au/find-help-for/independent-contractors/sham-contracting)","Fair Work Ombudsman - Sham contracting in the spotlight, media release March 2026 (https://www.fairwork.gov.au/newsroom/media-releases/2026-media-releases/march-2026/20260313-sham-contracting-media-release)","ATO - Sham contracting in the spotlight (https://www.ato.gov.au/media-centre/sham-contracting-in-the-spotlight)"}','If you are unsure whether someone is a contractor or an employee, or a contractor has drifted into regular hours for you alone, get advice before you engage or renew them. Misclassification can mean back-pay, per-breach civil penalties, and ATO super guarantee liability.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS14','federal','Check every new starter''s work rights in VEVO before their first shift, and check every one of them, not just the people you assume might be on a visa. VEVO is a Department of Home Affairs system that confirms visa status, work conditions and expiry, with the person''s consent. The Migration Act makes it an offence to employ someone without work rights, and penalties apply per worker. The trap is treating it as a one-off. Time-limited visas expire and carry conditions such as work-hour limits, so diary a re-check before each expiry date. Do not take their word for it, and do not assume a recruitment agency or labour hire firm has done it for you. Asking only candidates who ''look'' like migrants is itself discriminatory, so use the same wording and the same check for everyone.','{"Department of Home Affairs - Work-related contraventions (immi.homeaffairs.gov.au, Employing and sponsoring someone / Employer responsibilities)","Department of Home Affairs - Visa Entitlement Verification Online (VEVO)","Migration Act 1958 (Cth)","Migration Amendment (Strengthening Employer Compliance) Act 2024 (Cth)"}','Get migration or employment-law advice before you act if a check shows someone has no work rights, has breached a visa condition, or has a visa about to expire. Acting wrongly can mean ending employment unlawfully, and continuing can mean a per-worker offence. Also get advice if you have been checking only some candidates, because that is a discrimination risk.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS7','federal','Ask about the job, not the person. If the role needs weekend shifts, travel or physical work, say so and ask whether they can do it. Do not ask about age, pregnancy or family plans, kids, marital status, health, religion or where they are from. The Fair Work Act stops you refusing to employ someone because of attributes like these, and state and territory anti-discrimination laws add more, with the list varying by state. The trap is the friendly chat: "just making conversation" questions are where people slip. If you do not hire them, a question about their kids can look like the reason. Questions directly about the inherent requirements of the role are fine. Candidates can decline to answer. They can complain to the Fair Work Ombudsman, Fair Work Commission or Australian Human Rights Commission. Check AI-drafted or generic question banks before you use them.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth) s351","HRM Online - Illegal interview questions: be careful what you ask (https://www.hrmonline.com.au/recruitment/illegal-interview-questions/)","Hillhouse Legal Partners - Questions to avoid in a job interview (https://hillhouse.com.au/blog/questions-to-avoid-when-conducting-a-job-interview/)"}','If a candidate complains, or you have already asked a question about a protected attribute and then decided not to hire them, get advice before you respond. A refusal to employ linked to that attribute is adverse action and discrimination exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS8','federal','Before you interview, write down the four or five things the job actually needs, then write the same core questions for each one and ask every candidate those questions. Write down their examples during the interview, not from memory afterwards, and score each answer against the criteria before you compare people. Gut feel is the trap. ''Loved their energy'' or ''didn''t feel right'' are poor predictors of how someone will do the job, and they are where bias slips in most easily. They are also the hardest hiring decisions to defend. If a candidate you knocked back claims discrimination or adverse action, asking different people different questions with no written record leaves you nothing to show the decision was about the job. You can still have a friendly chat. Just do it on top of the structure, not instead of it.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Protection from discrimination at work"}','If a candidate who missed out raises discrimination or adverse action, or you suspect a protected attribute influenced the decision, get legal advice before you respond, because an unstructured interview with no notes is very hard to defend.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS16','federal','Before the person starts, put the offer in writing and make sure it covers the role, whether they are full-time, part-time or casual, start date, ordinary hours, location, pay, superannuation, probation terms and notice provisions. Give them the Fair Work Information Statement too, and the Casual Employment Information Statement if they are casual. A quick email confirming a start date and salary is the trap. It feels done, but it leaves gaps that surface later as a dispute, usually over notice, probation or entitlements. If you never set out probation or notice terms, you may find you cannot rely on them when it matters. Not handing over the Information Statement can itself be a breach. ''We''ll sort the formal contract later'' rarely happens, so send the full terms with the offer. Use a standard template, and check pay against your award.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Fair Work Information Statement","HQ.ai AU HR research report - G1. Offer letters and contracts missing essential terms"}','If a dispute has already started over notice, probation or entitlements for someone hired on an informal or verbal offer, get advice before you act. Relying on terms that were never written down can weaken your position or lead to a claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS1','federal','Before you open a single application, write down a short list of must-have criteria and a separate list of nice-to-haves, all tied to the actual job. Then screen against that list only. Anyone clearly missing a must-have goes in the no pile, and you read the rest properly. Set your cut-line by how many people you can realistically interview, not by how tired you are on Sunday night. The trap is reading first and working out what you want later. That is how screening turns into skimming for keywords, or rejecting someone over a typo or formatting that has nothing to do with the role. Keep a one-line note of why each person was cut. It takes seconds. If a rejected candidate later alleges discrimination, an unrecorded decision is hard to defend.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - A1. Resume overload with no triage system"}','If a rejected candidate complains, alleges discrimination, or asks why they were cut, get advice before you reply. How you screened and what you recorded will matter.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS3','federal','Before you open applications, write down the criteria for the role, split into essential and desirable, and decide how much each one counts. Then score every application against that same list and keep the scores. Most small businesses screen on gut feel. The problem is less your instinct than that you cannot explain it later. Because the same person often screens, interviews and decides, nobody else checks the reasoning. The trap is writing the criteria after you have already picked someone, or changing them halfway through. If a rejected applicant raises a discrimination or adverse-action complaint, a written rubric applied the same way to everyone is your main defence. Without one, a complaint becomes a real legal exposure. AI screening tools and new privacy rules are also adding pressure to explain screening decisions in writing.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - A3. No consistent scoring rubric for CV screening"}','If a rejected applicant complains, raises discrimination, or questions why they were not shortlisted, get advice before you respond or recreate any records. Writing criteria after the fact to justify a decision can turn a complaint into a real claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('X8','federal','Before acting on an HR answer, decide what kind of question it is. If it is general or factual, like what an entitlement is or how to calculate it, the Fair Work Ombudsman website and small business helpline are free and authoritative, and its Employer Advisory Service gives free tailored written advice. If it turns on your specific facts or the stakes are high, general resources cannot answer it for you. The trap is a confident source that is not qualified. Your bookkeeper can run payroll, but that is not employment-law expertise. A mate or a Facebook group may be describing a different state, award or situation. Some commercial ads have been found to mislead small businesses into thinking they were a government service, so check who you are talking to. Bad advice usually surfaces at claim or audit time, when it costs far more to fix.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - 6.8 Where SMEs Currently Go for Answers (Fragmented, Uneven-Quality Advice Sourcing)","Fair Work Ombudsman - Half a million calls from small business reveals strong demand for online support","Fair Work Commission - Workplace Advice Service"}','If the matter involves a dismissal, general protections, bullying or sexual harassment, or any fact-specific situation where a claim or back-pay is possible, do not rely on Google, forums or your bookkeeper - get tailored advice before you act. Eligible small businesses may get free legal help through the Fair Work Commission''s Workplace Advice Service.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('X5','federal','Before you call anyone, put the key facts, dates and documents on one page, then get two or three quotes or fixed-fee offers before you commit. Quotes are standard and cost nothing. Match the help to the question. Routine compliance questions often suit an HR consultant, which is usually much cheaper than a lawyer. A one-off question suits a single fixed-fee consult, while an ongoing need may suit a retainer. Ask whether a lawyer can review your document rather than handle the whole matter. If the amount in dispute is smaller than the cost of fighting it, settling may be the better business call. The trap is waiting until a problem feels big enough to justify the bill, because by then the cheap options have closed. The opposite trap is going it alone once a dismissal, investigation or underpayment is live.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - 6.5 Cost Barrier - Legal/Consultant Fees Are Prohibitive and Opaque"}','Once a matter involves a dismissal, investigation, discrimination or harassment complaint, or possible underpayment, get professional advice before you act. Saving on fees at that point routinely costs far more in penalties, back-pay or settlement.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('X6','federal','Sort HR tasks by risk before you sort them by urgency. Routine items like a standard leave request can be batched into a set time each week and handled from a template. Anything involving a termination, a complaint or an audit letter gets your full attention straight away. The trap is treating every HR question as equally urgent, so low-risk admin eats the same anxious energy as the jobs that can actually hurt you. The other trap is leaving compliance admin until a deadline or complaint forces it, because that is exactly when mistakes happen under time pressure. When a question comes up for the second time, build a standing process or template for it so you only solve it once. If a question is genuinely new, that is the one worth getting one-off advice on.','{"CCIWA - Big stress in small business: new survey reveals rising pressure (2025 Small Business Conditions Survey)"}','If the item on your desk is a termination, a complaint or an audit letter, it is not admin to batch. Get advice before you act, because a rushed mistake there creates legal exposure that saved time will not undo.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('X3','federal','Get advice before you act, not after a claim arrives. Routine paperwork, everyday performance conversations and standard leave questions are generally fine to handle yourself. Bring in a human professional if the matter could end in dismissal with disputed facts, or involves discrimination, a bullying or harassment complaint, a redundancy or restructure, or an enterprise agreement. The trap is that a performance chat and a disciplinary process heading towards dismissal look the same in the moment. Watch timing. Acting on a termination soon after they raise a complaint, a health issue, pregnancy, family responsibilities or a leave request is itself adverse action risk, whatever your real reason. Redundancy means the job no longer exists, not that you would rather they left, and the Small Business Fair Dismissal Code does not cover it. If a complaint names a senior person or you, use an external investigator.','{"Fair Work Commission - Dismissal rules for small business owners","Fair Work Commission - General protections and harmful (adverse) action","Safe Work Australia - Guide for Preventing and Responding to Workplace Bullying","Australian Human Rights Commission - Assistance and making a complaint"}','Any possible dismissal, redundancy, restructure, or discrimination, bullying or harassment complaint, and any adverse decision close in time to a complaint, leave, illness, injury or pregnancy - get advice from an employment lawyer or qualified HR practitioner before you act. Once a letter is sent or an informal chat has happened, your options narrow and claims are cheap to lodge.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('X7','federal','Before you act on anything that affects someone''s pay, job or safety, run it past a second, qualified source. Not knowing what you don''t know is normal when HR landed on you. The trap is that the regulator gives no discount for ''we didn''t know'', and silence from staff is not proof you are compliant. Sort your questions by stakes. Everyday questions about awards, leave and the Fair Work Act are often answered free by the Fair Work Ombudsman, so check there before paying for advice. Anything touching dismissal, discipline, pay you may have got wrong, complaints, pregnancy, illness or injury needs a qualified answer before you move, not a mate''s opinion or a forum post. Keep a short checklist and notes of past decisions so you rely less on memory and gut feel.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - fairwork.gov.au (free guidance on pay, awards and entitlements)","Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth)"}','Any decision about dismissal, discipline, possible underpayment, a complaint, pregnancy, illness or injury - get a qualified answer before you act, because a wrong move there cannot be undone by apologising.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('X1','federal','Pick one place where each employee''s records live and make it your official record this week. That covers their contract and any changes to it, timesheets, leave balances, and who agreed to what, when. It does not need to be expensive software, but it cannot be one person''s inbox or memory. The trap is that spreadsheets seem fine until a Fair Work audit, an underpayment claim or a dismissal dispute exposes every gap at once. Poor records weaken your position in a dispute. Fair Work Inspectors can issue infringement notices for record-keeping failures alone, separate from any underpayment. The Small Business Fair Dismissal Code checklist only protects you if it was actually completed and kept. Reconcile leave balances regularly and keep one current copy of each policy and contract. When award rates change, check your pay against the Fair Work Pay Calculator.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Infringement notices","Fair Work Ombudsman - Record-keeping","Fair Work Commission - Dismissal rules for small business owners","Hall & Wilcox - What every Australian business needs to know about employee records (hallandwilcox.com.au/news/what-every-australian-business-needs-to-know-about-employee-records/)","MDC Legal - Employers need to keep employment records (mdclegal.com.au/news/employers-need-to-keep-employment-records/)"}','If tidying your records shows pay or leave may not have matched the award, or the gap comes up during a Fair Work contact, underpayment claim or dismissal dispute, get advice before you correct anything. Mishandling the fix can turn a records problem into an underpayment or unfair dismissal exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('X2','federal','Start keeping records now, before anyone asks for them. The Fair Work Act requires you to make and keep employee records for seven years, covering pay, hours, leave, superannuation and more. Beyond that, write a short dated note after every performance conversation, disciplinary meeting or verbal warning. A verbal warning is not informal just because it was spoken. If you dismiss someone and you are a small business, complete the Small Business Fair Dismissal Code checklist at the time, not after a claim arrives, because a checklist filled in later carries much less weight. For any redundancy, keep a record of the consultation and the redeployment options you considered. Keep old contract versions and your award-rate calculations too. The trap is that nobody tells you until an inspector or tribunal asks, and by then missing records usually count against you.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 s535","Fair Work Ombudsman - Small Business Fair Dismissal Code"}','Get advice before you act if records are missing or incomplete and you are facing a dismissal, redundancy, unfair dismissal claim, underpayment query or Fair Work Ombudsman request. Do not backfill or recreate documents after the fact, because missing records can decide the matter against you.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF4','federal','If someone works for you as a contractor, check that the arrangement is real in practice, not just on paper. Sham contracting is misrepresenting what is really employment as an independent contracting arrangement, usually in a way that avoids award, NES and super obligations. It is a separate contravention from simple misclassification, and it carries its own penalties. The trap is thinking you are safe because you never meant to do it. Recklessness counts, and whether your belief was reasonable is judged objectively. A signed contractor agreement is not a complete defence, and neither is ''everyone in my industry does it this way''. Review long-standing contractor relationships, especially ones that have changed over time. Penalties are significant, and there can be flow-on super and tax exposure. The regulator has named construction and road transport as priority sectors.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 s357","Fair Work Ombudsman - Nearly $200,000 in penalties for sham contracting involving workers with disability (media release, 30 July 2024)","Fair Work Ombudsman - Sham contracting in the spotlight (media release, 13 March 2026)","Department of Employment and Workplace Relations - Compliance and enforcement: Civil penalties and sham contracting factsheet"}','If a worker challenges their contractor status, or you are deciding whether to reclassify or end any contractor arrangement, get legal advice before you act. A wrong move can turn a classification problem into penalties, back-pay and super exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF7','federal','When a casual tells you in writing they want to go permanent, note the date and reply in writing within the deadline the Fair Work Act sets. Missing that deadline is the trap. You no longer have to review casuals and offer conversion yourself. Under the Employee Choice Pathway the employee starts it. They become eligible after six months, or twelve months if you have fewer than 15 employees. You can say no, but only on valid grounds. Examples are the person still genuinely meeting the casual definition, or conversion needing substantial changes to how you run the business. ''I''d rather keep her casual'' is not a ground. Write your reasons down and think about talking with them before you refuse. Check the current response period on the Fair Work Ombudsman site. Getting it wrong can mean civil penalties and a Fair Work Commission dispute.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 Part 2-2 Division 4A (casual employment - employee choice)","Fair Work Act 2009 s15A","Fair Work Ombudsman - Casual employees","Master Builders ACT - Employee Choice Pathway - Casual to Permanent Conversion","DLA Piper - New casual conversion changes are now in force"}','If you plan to refuse, the deadline is close, or the employee disputes your reasons or raises it with the Fair Work Commission, get advice before you reply. A late or poorly grounded refusal carries civil penalty exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF2','federal','Before you offer or renew a fixed-term contract, check two things: will the total time with this person pass two years, and is this already a renewal? Generally a fixed-term contract cannot run past two years including renewals, cannot be renewed more than once, and cannot roll over automatically. Exceptions exist, such as genuine seasonal work, specific-task funding and high-income employees, but the list is narrow and technical, so do not assume you fit. The trap is back-to-back contracts: earlier contracts with the same person count towards the cap. A term that breaks the rules has no effect, and the job may become ongoing by law. The Fair Work Act requires you to give the Fixed Term Contract Information Statement with every new fixed-term contract. Using fixed terms to sidestep redundancy or NES obligations is a target, so if the work is ongoing, offer an ongoing job.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Fixed term contract employees","Fair Work Ombudsman - New Fixed Term Contract Information Statement","Fair Work Act 2009 ss333E-333N"}','If the person is already past two years or a second renewal, you are relying on an exception, or you plan to let a contract end instead of renewing it, get advice before you act. The job may already be ongoing by law, so ending it could be a dismissal or redundancy.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF16','federal','Treat this as two separate things. The NES gives eligible staff up to 12 months'' unpaid parental leave, and they can ask for a further 12 months. The government Paid Parental Leave scheme is run by Services Australia. You do not fund it, though in some cases you may be asked to pass the government payment through your payroll. You do not have to pay them yourself while they are away unless your own policy offers paid leave on top. Super on government Paid Parental Leave is paid by the ATO, not through your payroll. Topping up super during unpaid leave is your call. The trap is the return. They have a right to come back to the same or an equivalent role, and refusing leave or failing to honour that right breaches the NES. Check the notice and evidence steps, and check Services Australia for the current scheme length.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 ss67-85","Services Australia - Paid Parental Leave scheme changes","ATO - Paid Parental Leave Superannuation Contribution","Department of Social Services - Super on Paid Parental Leave and expansion of the scheme"}','Any refusal of parental leave, any change to their role on return, or any discipline or dismissal around pregnancy or parental leave needs legal advice before you act. Getting it wrong risks an NES contravention and a general protections or discrimination claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF17','federal','Work out which state or territory the person mainly works in, then use that state''s long service leave law, not the Fair Work Act. Long service leave is not part of the NES. Each state and territory has its own law, and they differ on how many years someone needs before they qualify, how much leave they get, when a part payout is owed if they leave early, and what pay the leave is calculated on. Where the work is mainly done generally decides which law applies, not where your head office is. A remote worker interstate may be under different rules from the rest of your team. The trap is applying one state''s rules to everyone, or missing a pro-rata payout when someone resigns or is made redundant before the full qualifying period. Check the relevant state law before you pay anything out.','{"Long Service Leave Act 1955 (NSW)","Long Service Leave Act 2018 (Vic)","Industrial Relations Act 2016 (Qld) Chapter 2 Part 3"}','Any termination, resignation or redundancy of someone with several years'' service, or any worker based in a different state from you, needs a check of that state''s law and payout rules before final pay. Getting the pro-rata entitlement or base rate wrong is an underpayment with back-pay and state penalty exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF19','federal','If someone tells you they need time off to deal with family and domestic violence, give it to them and keep it quiet. All employees, including casuals, can take 10 days of paid family and domestic violence leave in a 12-month period. The full 10 days are available upfront, not built up over time, and unused days do not roll over to the next year. The trap is the payslip. Do not write ''family and domestic violence leave'' on it. Show the time as ordinary hours or another neutral description, because naming it is a separate breach of the confidentiality rules. You can ask for some evidence, but keep it limited and do not demand more than you need. In a small team you may already know their situation, so be careful about who else learns anything. Check the Fair Work Ombudsman fact sheet for what evidence is reasonable.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 ss106A-106E","Fair Work Ombudsman - About family and domestic violence leave","Fair Work Ombudsman - Family and domestic violence leave fact sheet","Department of Employment and Workplace Relations - Paid family and domestic violence leave resources now available to support small business employers"}','If you are thinking of refusing the leave, the employee''s safety or injury is involved, or their details may already have been disclosed, get advice before you act. Denying the leave or breaching payslip confidentiality each carry civil penalty exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF12','federal','If you pay an award-covered employee a flat salary, keep a record of the hours they actually work and check the salary against what the award would have paid. A generous salary does not cover everything on its own. Many awards, including the Clerks award, have an annualised wage clause. Where it applies, you need to write down which award entitlements the salary is meant to cover, set an outer limit on the hours or penalties it absorbs, and reconcile at least once a year. If the salary fell short, pay the difference. Skipping the reconciliation is the trap: failing to reconcile or keep records is a breach in itself, separate from any shortfall. A contractual set-off clause is only an option where your award allows it, and the wording has to be precise. Check your award''s own clause before relying on either.','{"Corrs Chambers Westgarth - Annualised salaries: what are the options? https://www.corrs.com.au/insights/annualised-salaries-what-are-the-options","Parke Lawyers - Annualised Salaries and Modern Awards: Common Mistakes Employers Make https://pl.com.au/information-centre/annualised-salaries-modern-awards","Holding Redlich - Key changes to modern award annualised salaries https://www.holdingredlich.com/key-changes-to-modern-award-annualised-salaries"}','If a reconciliation shows a shortfall, hours were never recorded, or you are relying on a set-off clause you are not sure your award supports, get advice before you act - this is underpayment exposure and the back-pay obligation does not go away.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF23','federal','Pay from the hours people actually worked, not the roster. Keep a record of real start and finish times for every shift, including the unrostered late finish. That one entry can decide whether overtime or a penalty rate was owed at all. Trusting your staff is fine, but trust is not a record. The Fair Work Act sets a record-keeping obligation, and correct award pay, overtime, penalties and super all depend on the hours data underneath. When the roster and actual hours don''t match, check and correct before the pay run, and talk to the person about any disagreement. The trap is rounding. Rounding that always lands in your favour is a specific red flag for the Fair Work Ombudsman. Estimates and gaps also make any later dispute about hours much harder for you.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Record-keeping and pay slips fact sheet","Fair Work Act 2009 s535"}','If you find past pays were calculated from rostered rather than actual hours, or an employee disputes the hours you paid, get advice before you fix it. That is now a possible underpayment, and how you handle it matters.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF28','federal','If you pay employees, report each pay run to the ATO through STP-enabled payroll software. A spreadsheet or manual payroll will not do it. STP applies to essentially all employers regardless of size, and every pay run is its own reporting event, so it is not something you tidy up once a year. Each report sends the ATO salary and wages, PAYG withholding and super information, and it replaces the old end-of-year payment summaries. The trap is thinking reporting is paying. STP is an information obligation only. You still have to pay the PAYG withholding and the super separately. If you find a mistake in a report you have already lodged, correct it promptly rather than leaving it. Missing or wrong reports can bring penalties and a higher chance of an audit and back-payments.','{"Taxation Administration Act 1953 (Cth)"}','If you have missed STP reports, cannot correct a lodged report, or have reported PAYG or super you have not actually paid, speak to your accountant or registered tax or BAS agent before you fix it. Unpaid super or withholding can mean back-payments and penalties.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM16','federal','When someone complains, act promptly and write the complaint down. Then talk to the complainant, the person accused and any witnesses separately, and take notes. Give the accused the specific allegation and a genuine chance to respond before you decide anything. Weigh the evidence, reach a finding on what is more likely than not, tell both people the outcome, and follow up to check it has settled. The trap is acting on the first account you hear. Doing nothing is just as risky: bullying and conflict are a recognised psychosocial hazard under WHS laws, which vary by state. If you are close to either person, or are one of them, be honest about that and consider whether someone more independent should run it. A sloppy process can also undermine any later discipline or dismissal that relies on it.','{"Safe Work Australia - Psychosocial hazards"}','Get advice before you act if the complaint involves harassment, sexual harassment, discrimination or a protected attribute, if you are one of the parties, or if the findings could lead to discipline or dismissal. A mishandled process can become a WHS or general protections claim in its own right.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM18','federal','Keep a simple record of every absence: date, day of the week, reason given, and whether it was paid leave. Without a system, patterns like Mondays, Fridays or days after a public holiday are easy to feel and hard to prove. If a pattern shows up, have a private, supportive conversation first and ask how they are going, not why they keep letting you down. Look for what might sit behind it, such as workload, caring duties, health or stress at work. The trap is treating genuine sick or carer''s leave as a conduct problem. That leave is a protected NES entitlement, and penalising it risks a general protections claim. Unauthorised absence is different and can be managed. If absence points to overwork or another hazard, your state or territory WHS duty applies. Only move to a formal process if the pattern continues after support is offered.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 Part 2-2 Division 7 (NES personal/carer''s leave)","Fair Work Act 2009 ss340-361 (general protections)"}','Before any warning, discipline or formal process over absence, or where the absences involve illness, injury, mental health, caring duties or a possible workplace hazard, get advice from an HR or employment-law professional. Treating protected leave as misconduct creates general protections exposure that cannot be undone.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM13','federal','Yes, this applies to you now, even if you have fewer than 15 staff. The small business exemption has ended. It is not a ban on after-hours contact. You can still text about tomorrow''s shift. What changes is that staff can refuse to monitor, read or respond to work contact outside their hours, unless that refusal is unreasonable. Whether it is unreasonable depends on the facts: why you made contact, how you made it, how urgent it was, the person''s role, whether they are paid to be available, and their personal circumstances. The trap is leaving ''reasonable'' undefined. Sit down with your team and agree what after-hours contact looks like for each role, such as who gets rostering texts and what counts as urgent. Then write it down, as a policy or agreed norms. If a disagreement can''t be sorted out, it can go to the Fair Work Commission.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - New right to disconnect laws","Fair Work Ombudsman - Right to disconnect for small business employees starts 26 August"}','If an employee has refused after-hours contact and you are thinking of taking any action against them because of it, or a dispute has gone to the Fair Work Commission, get advice before you act. Treating a refusal as misconduct without checking whether it was unreasonable can turn a rostering disagreement into a claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM2','federal','Before they start, send the contract, the Fair Work Information Statement and, for casuals, the Casual Employment Information Statement, and set up their systems access. Then write down what good looks like in the role: a plan for the first week, a 30-day check-in with goals, a 60-day mid-point and a 90-day review timed to the end of probation. Keep a short note of each conversation. A buddy is fine, but it does not replace you checking in. The trap is that onboarding feels optional when you are busy, so it gets cut. If performance or conduct later becomes a problem, you will have nothing written to point to, and a warning, performance plan or dismissal becomes much harder to show was fair. Missing the information statements is a breach in its own right, and it weakens your position in any later dispute.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Before starting employment"}','If a new starter''s performance or conduct is already a concern and you are thinking about a warning, performance plan or ending their employment, especially near the end of probation, get advice before you act. Unfair dismissal and general protections claims are hard to defend without written expectations and a fair process.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM11','federal','Work out how long they have been continuously employed, then check the NES notice scale against their award and contract and use whichever is longer. NES notice rises with service up to four weeks, plus one extra week if they are over 45 with at least two years'' service. Casuals get no notice. You can have them work the notice or pay it out instead. Then add any accrued annual leave they have not taken. The trap is thinking small business means no notice: small businesses are exempt from redundancy pay, not notice. When final pay must be paid can vary by state or territory, so check that too. Get it wrong and a clean termination becomes an underpayment claim, with back-pay and super on top. Check the amounts with your award or the Fair Work Pay Calculator.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Notice of termination and redundancy pay fact sheet","Fair Work Ombudsman - Dismissal","Fair Work Act 2009 s117"}','Get advice before you act if the termination is for serious misconduct without notice, if the dismissal itself could be challenged, or if you find a final pay was already underpaid. A notice or leave error adds an underpayment claim on top of any dismissal claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM15','federal','If you are a director or owner who makes the big decisions, assume you are an ''officer'' and that you personally carry a due diligence duty, separate from the business''s own WHS duty. That means taking active steps, not trusting that someone has it covered. For a small business the basics are a simple safety policy, a way for staff to report hazards and incidents, emergency procedures, risk assessments for higher-risk tasks, and genuine consultation with workers. Consultation can be informal, but you have to actually seek their input and consider it. The trap is writing a policy once and never checking it is followed. Stay current on your WHS knowledge and regularly check the system is actually being used. The duty sits in your state or territory WHS Act, so details and penalties vary, and officers can be personally liable.','{"Safe Work Australia - What does an officer need to do?","Model Work Health and Safety Act s27 (officer due diligence) and state or territory equivalents"}','Get legal advice as soon as there is a serious incident, injury, or contact from your WHS regulator. Officers can be personally liable, and in some states the most serious cases can lead to imprisonment.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA8','federal','Match the channel to the role before you spend anything. For high-volume or entry-level roles, start with free or low-cost job boards. For specialist or niche roles, targeted channels such as industry boards or professional networking sites may earn their cost. The trap is defaulting to the most expensive channel for every role, then posting once and giving up. Treat your first ad as a test. If the right people are not applying, change the wording or the channel mix and try again. On hard-to-fill roles where you are competing for candidates, budget for paid visibility upgrades. Estimate how much of your own time the hire will take, so you know when an agency fee is worth it. Treat published cost comparisons with caution, because most come from job boards and agencies. Whichever channel you use, the same rules about what your ad says still apply.','{"SEEK Employer - Hiring advice: Hiring trends to watch (https://talent.seek.com.au/hiring-advice/article/hiring-trends-to-watch)","Appetency Recruitment - Best Hiring Channels: Where to Advertise Jobs in Australia (https://www.appetencyrecruitment.com.au/blog/best-recruitment-channels-for-small-businesses-where-to-advertise-jobs-in-australia)","ScaleSuite - Hiring Costs by Channel: LinkedIn vs Agencies vs Referrals (Australia) (https://www.scalesuite.com.au/resources/hiring-cost-by-channel-australia)"}','If you are unsure whether your ad wording could exclude people on a protected attribute such as age, sex or family responsibilities, get it checked before it goes live, because discrimination rules apply on every channel. If you are advertising because you plan to sponsor a visa holder, stop and get migration advice first, because sponsorship requires evidence of a genuine shortage for the role.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RA15','federal','Decide your negotiation room before the ad goes live, so the advertised figure is not your absolute ceiling. Know your top number before a candidate asks, not while they are waiting on the phone. Negotiating after an offer is now normal, so expect it rather than reading it as a bad sign. If their current employer counter-offers, you have three choices: match, hold firm, or walk away. In shortage roles, plan for this early so you do not lose a signed candidate at the last minute. The trap is the one-off exception. Before you go up for one person, check what your existing staff doing similar work are paid, and write down why this offer is different. Ad hoc, undocumented pay decisions are the same habit that creates equal-remuneration exposure later.','{"Robert Half - 2026 Australia Salary Guide / negotiation insights - https://www.roberthalf.com/au/en/insights/career-development/how-negotiate-higher-salary-offer-email-sample-included"}','Get advice before going ahead if the offer would put a new starter above existing staff doing similar work, especially where the gap falls along gender lines. Undocumented one-off pay decisions are what equal-remuneration claims are built on.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA4','federal','Before you post the ad, split your list into two columns: true must-haves and nice-to-haves. Must-haves are the safety, legal and technical minimums the job cannot be done without. Everything else is a nice-to-have. Then ask whether the list is really one role or secretly two, and check it against the pay you can actually offer. This is the small-business trap: you can only afford one hire, so you try to fix every gap with one person. Asking for ten years'' experience on a junior wage, or a mix of tools and tickets that rarely come together, leaves the role empty while you pass over good people who tick most boxes. Inflated requirements can also tip into indirect discrimination if they are not genuine requirements of the job and they screen out a protected group, such as unnecessary physical, language or experience thresholds.','{"SEEK Employer - Unicorns: Who Makes the Ideal Candidates? (https://sg.employer.seek.com/hiring-advice/article/unicorn-candidates)","GWG Blog - How to spot (and secure) a unicorn candidate (https://gwg.com.au/blog/how-to-spot-and-secure-a-unicorn-candidate/)"}','If a requirement could screen out people by age, sex, disability, race, language or another protected attribute and you cannot show the job genuinely needs it, get advice before you advertise or shortlist. A requirement that is not genuinely needed for the job can create indirect discrimination exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA16','federal','Before you set a budget or timeline, check whether the role is on the Jobs and Skills Australia Occupation Shortage List, and think about how your region compares. Hiring has got easier overall, with more applications per ad, but health, trades, early childhood and school teaching have stayed tight. That is the trap. Commentary about corporate or tech hiring tells you little about finding an electrician or a nurse. For a shortage role, allow more time to fill and review your offer, including pay and what else makes the job worth taking. Think about widening your search by region, skills-based hiring or visa sponsorship. For a role with plenty of applicants, expect volume and put your effort into screening rather than advertising. Either way, do not let urgency push you into skipping proper screening or right-to-work checks.','{"Jobs and Skills Australia - 2025 Occupation Shortage List","Australian Bureau of Statistics - Labour Force, Australia (latest release)"}','Get specialist migration or employment advice before you make an offer if you are thinking about visa sponsorship or are unsure how to check someone''s right to work. Mistakes there are hard to undo once the person has started.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RA18','federal','Treat a hard-to-fill role as a project with a deadline, not something that will turn up. Decide early whether it is a shortage or niche role, especially in a regional area, because those take much longer than the national median of roughly a month. If it is, put in more effort and budget from day one: turn ads around fast and book interviews quickly. At the same time, set an interim cover plan, such as overtime, a temp, or redistributing work. Then set a date when you will widen channels, lift spend or bring in an agency if the role is still open. The trap is treating months of cover as business as usual. Sustained understaffing raises fatigue and safety risk and can push good staff to resign, sometimes with a claim attached. Check in regularly with whoever is covering.','{"SEEK Employer - Hiring trends to watch (https://talent.seek.com.au/hiring-advice/article/hiring-trends-to-watch)","SmartCompany - Wrong hires costing Australian SMEs, SEEK research, Feb 2026 (https://www.smartcompany.com.au/people-human-resources/wrong-hires-costing-australian-smes-7-3-billion-year-seek/)"}','If staff covering the gap report fatigue, have a safety incident or injury, or say they are leaving because of the workload, get advice before you act. Sustained understaffing creates WHS exposure and can lead to resignation or constructive dismissal claims.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA1','federal','Before you advertise, pause long enough to check the need is real. Work through it in order: confirm the work will keep coming, list the essential tasks that must be covered, decide whether you need temporary cover or a permanent hire, then decide whether the role should keep its old shape or be redesigned. Last, check your cash flow can carry an ongoing wage. The trap is auto-backfilling. The person who left may have been in a role that was never right-sized, and replacing them can buy time without fixing a broken process. Ask whether this is a hiring problem or a systems problem. Also check the hours: the real gap is sometimes 15 to 20 hours a week, not a full-time job. A rushed, poorly scoped role can later cause underpayment, sham contracting or discrimination problems.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - A1. Deciding whether to hire at all (backfill vs redesign vs absorb vs automate)"}','Get advice before you act if your plan changes an existing employee''s duties or hours, removes or merges a role, or covers the work with a contractor instead of an employee. A wrong move there can turn into a redundancy, underpayment or sham contracting claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS23','federal','List every point in your hiring process where software screens out candidates, ranks them, or decides who gets an interview. A new Privacy Act rule requires your privacy policy to say what kinds of personal information feed those automated decisions and what kinds of decisions they are. The privacy regulator is reading ''automated decision'' broadly, and deciding who gets an interview is likely in scope. The trap is thinking the vendor carries this. It does not sit with them alone, so ask the vendor in writing what candidate data the tool uses and what it decides. While you are at it, stop collecting data ''in case it''s useful'', store what you keep securely, and set a deletion schedule for rejected candidates, including video and audio recordings. Update the policy before the rule starts, and confirm the start date on the OAIC website.','{"Office of the Australian Information Commissioner - Guidance on privacy and the use of commercially available AI products","Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) Sch 1 - Australian Privacy Principles, APP 1 (new APP 1.7-1.9 automated decision-making transparency)","HQ.ai AU HR research report - I2. Privacy Act obligations for AI recruitment tools and the new ADM transparency rule"}','Get privacy advice before you publish the updated privacy policy, and straight away if you are unsure whether the Privacy Act applies to your business, cannot get clear answers from your vendor about what the tool uses and decides, a candidate complains about an automated assessment, or candidate data is lost or breached. If a screening tool may be filtering people out on a protected attribute, that is also discrimination exposure and needs a human to look at it.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS19','federal','Set up a basic automated status update so every applicant hears something, even if it is only ''we received your application'' and ''this role has been filled''. Then shorten the gap between final interview and signed offer, and check in with your new starter a few days before day one to confirm they are still coming. Ghosting now runs both ways. Candidates apply with one click, treat any single application lightly, and will take a faster offer without telling you. Employers with big applicant pools often reply to almost nobody. The trap is thinking silence costs you nothing. In a small local labour market it hurts your reputation and can turn up in online reviews. Keep a simple record of who applied and what you decided, so you can show your process was fair if anyone questions it.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - H1. Two-way candidate ghosting"}','If an unsuccessful applicant says your process was unfair, or links their rejection to a personal attribute such as age, pregnancy, disability or family responsibilities, stop and get advice before you reply. A pattern of never replying, with no record of why you decided, makes a fair process harder to show.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RS20','federal','Before you advertise, decide who can make the final call, how many interview rounds you genuinely need, and the longest gap you will allow between stages. Then stick to it. Most SMEs lose good candidates to a process that drags, not to a bad offer. Usually only the owner can sign off, interviews get bumped by the day job, and nobody is chasing it along. Run approvals side by side rather than one after another. If you cannot get everyone in a room, give one person authority to decide. When you make a verbal offer, have the written offer ready to follow quickly. The trap is treating a strong candidate as safe. They are the one most likely to take a faster offer, so move with more urgency, not less.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - H2. Slow time-to-hire losing candidates to faster movers"}','This is mainly a commercial efficiency issue. If the pressure to move fast tempts you to make an offer before the pay, classification or contract terms are settled, stop and check those first, because a rushed offer can lock in the wrong terms.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RS21','federal','Before you call an agency, work out the full cost of the hire, not just the fee. Add the placement fee, your own time briefing and interviewing, and the cost of onboarding the person once they start. Then decide role by role. Simpler roles you can often fill yourself through job boards, while senior or hard-to-fill roles may justify paying someone else to search. If you do use an agency, negotiate the replacement guarantee before you sign. That is the trap. Many owners only read that clause after a placement falls over, then pay again to repeat the process. Ask how long the guarantee runs and exactly what it covers. Use a specialist agency for a specialised role, not a generalist. This is commercial risk rather than legal risk, but read the agency contract properly before you commit.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - H3. Cost-per-hire pressure and agency-fee dependency"}','If the agency terms of business include large upfront or non-refundable fees, long exclusivity, or fees triggered if you later hire a candidate directly, have the contract reviewed before signing. A poorly understood agency contract can lock you into paying twice for one role.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RS12','federal','Write one short reference script and use it for every candidate for the role. Ask the same job-relevant questions the same way, and note what was actually said. Decide how many referees you need, and require that at least one is a direct former manager, so you are not only hearing from people the candidate hand-picked. Expect little. Many referees will only confirm dates and job title, because both sides worry about defamation if they say something negative they cannot back up. The trap is filling that silence with a ''vibe'' and letting it decide the hire. Base your decision on documented, factual content, and record how the references fed into it. A fair, consistent process is what makes your hiring decision defensible. When you are the referee, stick to facts you can substantiate.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - F1. Reference checks: legal risk and low-value information"}','If a referee makes serious allegations about a candidate, or you are asked to give a negative reference about a former employee, get advice before you rely on it or say it. Unsubstantiated negative statements create defamation exposure for both employers and referees.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RS13','federal','Before you order a police check, decide whether this role actually needs one, and write down why. Do not run checks on everyone just because a screening package bundles them in by default. Get the candidate''s consent before you run any check. If a record comes back, look at that specific offence against the inherent requirements of this specific role, and record your reasoning. ''Any record means no job'' is the trap. It feels safer, but automatically excluding someone without looking at relevance can be unlawful discrimination in some states and territories, where criminal record is a protected attribute. It can also support a general protections claim. Whether criminal record is protected depends on where you operate, so check your state or territory anti-discrimination law before relying on a blanket policy.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - F2. Police/criminal history checks used as a blunt, overreaching filter"}','Before you withdraw an offer or reject a candidate because of something on their police check, get advice. Discrimination and general protections exposure depends on your state or territory and on how relevant the offence is to the role.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS9','federal','Have every panellist score the candidate on their own, in writing, before anyone talks. Then compare scores and discuss the gaps. Keep the scorecard short: four to six competencies tied to the actual job, each rated on an anchored four-point scale where every point describes what that level looks like. A four-point scale removes the middle option people hide in. Add an evidence field under each score and require a specific example of what the candidate said or did. Drop vague items like ''culture fit'' as a standalone criterion. The trap is talking it through first. In a small panel, the owner or most senior person speaks and everyone else drifts towards their view. A well-designed scorecard used the same way for every candidate is a practical defence if a hiring decision is later challenged as discriminatory. A sloppy one only looks rigorous.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - D3. Interview scorecard design and panel calibration"}','If an unsuccessful candidate complains or alleges discrimination in the hiring decision, get advice before you respond. Keep the original individual scorecards and evidence notes, and do not rewrite them.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS17','federal','Treat probation as a check-in point in the contract, not a legal shield. Probation is a contractual idea that the Fair Work Act does not define or require. For unfair dismissal, what counts is the minimum employment period: generally six months, or 12 months if you are a small business with fewer than 15 employees. Your probation length and that period often do not line up. The trap is thinking "they''re still on probation, so we can just let them go." General protections apply from day one. If you end someone''s employment for a prohibited reason, they can bring a claim no matter how new they are or whether probation has finished. During probation, give honest feedback, keep notes, and base any decision on performance or conduct. When they pass, confirm it in writing so nobody is unsure where they stand.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - G2. Probation period misunderstanding","Fair Work Act 2009 ss340-361","Fair Work Act 2009 s383"}','If you are thinking of ending employment during probation, get advice before you act. This matters most if the person has recently raised a concern, taken leave, been ill or injured, or told you they are pregnant, because general protections claims apply regardless of length of service.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS18','federal','Once you have picked someone, get a written offer out as soon as you can after the final interview, and sort approvals before you interview, not after. Speed is the trap. Every day the offer waits for sign-off gives their current employer time to counter. Build some negotiation room into the offer upfront, and ask early what else matters to them, because flexibility and development often count for more than salary. If they still pull out, let them go gracefully and keep your runner-up warm. The same logic applies when a valued person resigns from you. A counter-offer rarely fixes why they wanted to leave, and research suggests most people who accept one still move on within about two years. Fix the root cause instead. Keep offers and acceptances in writing so nobody is unsure whether a deal was done.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - G3. Counter-offers and lost candidates at the eleventh hour"}','Get advice before you act if there is a dispute about whether an offer was accepted, or if you want to withdraw an offer someone has already accepted. Drawn-out negotiations can blur whether a binding agreement exists.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RS26','federal','Before they start, run one short checklist that turns the candidate into an employee: signed contract, TFN declaration, super choice form, the Fair Work Information Statement, and their IT and access set up. Then carry everything you learnt in recruitment into their employee record. That means reference notes, the start terms you agreed, and any adjustment you promised. On day one, do a real induction that covers WHS and emergency procedures, not just a chat and a tour. Book the 30, 60 and 90-day check-ins and the probation review now. ''We''ll sort the paperwork on day one'' is the trap. Each late or missing form is a separate compliance gap. A promised adjustment that gets lost once they start can create fresh discrimination exposure. Early problems are cheapest to fix when you spot them in the first few weeks.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - J1. Weak handoff from recruitment to onboarding/People function"}','Get advice before you act if you promised an adjustment during recruitment and now cannot or will not provide it, because withdrawing it can create discrimination exposure. Do the same if you find super or other paperwork was missed after the person has already been paid, because a missed obligation may need fixing properly rather than quietly.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS5','federal','Write a short script and use it for every candidate. Cover the same must-haves each time: availability, salary expectations, notice period, right to work, and the one or two non-negotiables for the role. Have the same person run the screens where you can, and jot down each answer straight after the call. A freeform ''quick chat to suss them out'' feels efficient. But it makes candidates hard to compare fairly and lets rapport decide who goes through. The trap is availability. Asking about kids or caring responsibilities to work out whether someone can do the hours drifts into family status and discrimination risk. Questions about age and health carry the same risk. Ask the job question instead: ''The role needs Saturday shifts. Can you do that?'' Keep the phone screen to basic fit, and save deeper questions for a structured interview.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - C1. Inconsistent, unstructured phone screens"}','If a candidate complains that they were asked about family, age, health or similar, or that they were screened out for one of those reasons, get advice before you respond. Discrimination exposure applies at every hiring stage, including an informal phone call.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS2','federal','Do not let the tool make the final call. Keep a person reviewing the shortlist, and have someone check the candidates the tool screened out, not just the ones it ranked highly. These tools learn from past hiring patterns and can filter people out on things that stand in for race, disability, age or sex, such as names, employment gaps, postcode or graduation year. The trap is trusting the vendor''s "bias-free" claim. If the outcome is discriminatory, the law still holds you responsible, even if it was unintentional and the bias came from someone else''s software. Federal discrimination laws and the Fair Work Act''s adverse action rules all apply. Ask the vendor for testing and audit evidence. Tell candidates you use AI in screening. Work through the AHRC''s AI and recruitment checklist before you rely on the tool.','{"Australian Human Rights Commission - AI and recruitment compliance checklist","Office of the Australian Information Commissioner - Guidance on privacy and the use of commercially available AI products","Racial Discrimination Act 1975 (Cth)","Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth)","Age Discrimination Act 2004 (Cth)","Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (Cth)","Fair Work Act 2009 s351","HQ.ai AU HR research report - A2. AI-assisted resume screening and algorithmic bias"}','If a candidate complains they were screened out unfairly, or you suspect the tool is filtering by name, age, gaps or postcode, get advice before responding. A discriminatory outcome creates liability even when the bias came from the vendor''s software.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS4','federal','Before you cut anyone, write down the criteria you are shortlisting against, and make them match the essential criteria in your ad. Then check every applicant against the same list and note one job-related reason for each person you drop. Skipping that record is the trap. Shortlisting is where ''why wasn''t I even interviewed?'' complaints start. Anti-discrimination law and the Fair Work Act''s general protections apply at this stage, not just at dismissal. A rejected candidate can bring a claim over being screened out, and your only real defence is showing a consistent, criteria-based process. ''Not a culture fit'' or ''I could just tell from the CV'' is not a reason. Hold referred or known candidates to the same standard as strangers. If you can, get a second person to look at borderline cases before you decide.','{"Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth) s351","Fair Work Ombudsman - Workplace discrimination fact sheet","HQ.ai AU HR research report - B1. Undocumented, indefensible shortlisting decisions"}','If a candidate complains, alleges discrimination or threatens a claim about not being shortlisted, get advice before you respond. General protections and discrimination claims apply to hiring decisions, and what you say next can become evidence.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('X9','federal','Before you sign up for paid HR support, count how many HR questions you had last year and buy for that, not for the worst case. There are three options: paying a lawyer by the hour for specific matters, paying an HR consultant by the hour or on a monthly retainer, and fixed-fee subscription services that bundle ''unlimited advice''. Hourly billing gets expensive if you ask lots of small questions. Retainers assume a monthly need that many small businesses do not have. Subscriptions can lock you into long contracts, so check what ''unlimited'' actually covers and read the term and exit clauses before you sign. The opposite trap is having no support and paying emergency rates every time, which over a year can cost more than a modest arrangement. Casual-heavy, high-turnover or multi-award workforces usually justify more structured help.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - 6.9 The Economics of Outsourced HR / Employment Legal Advice (What the Product Displaces)"}','If the question in front of you involves a formal claim, a complaint, or a letter from a lawyer or union, pay for human advice now rather than weighing cost. Saving on fees is a false economy once legal exposure is live.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('X4','federal','Standard job ads, interviews and offers are generally fine to handle yourself. Bring in a professional for three things. First, a candidate who needs visa sponsorship. Migration law then sits on top of employment law, and common failures are lodging without a valid skills assessment or without evidence of a genuine labour shortage for the role. Talk to an immigration lawyer before you lodge anything. Second, a role or hiring criteria that could exclude people on a protected attribute such as age or family responsibilities, even by accident. Have the ad checked before it goes live. Third, an enterprise agreement. If a franchisor, industry body or adviser suggests one, treat it as a multi-month specialist process with bargaining, a vote and Fair Work Commission approval, not a fancier contract to draft yourself. The trap is treating sponsorship as paperwork: errors can mean a refused visa or losing your sponsor approval.','{"Fair Work Commission - Make an enterprise agreement","Fair Work Commission - Approval of enterprise agreements","HQ.ai AU HR research report - 6.4 The Escalation Boundary - Recruitment Track (Visa Sponsorship, Discriminatory Hiring, Enterprise Agreements)"}','Any candidate who needs visa sponsorship, any suggestion that you make an enterprise agreement, or hiring criteria that could exclude people on a protected attribute - stop and get advice from an immigration lawyer, employment lawyer or qualified HR practitioner before you act. Sponsorship errors can mean visa refusal or loss of sponsor approval, on top of any employment-law exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF5','federal','Do not draft or issue an opt-out notice yourself. It must come from the contractor, in writing, and it is only available if their earnings are above the contractor high income threshold. Check the current threshold on the Fair Work Commission site rather than relying on last year''s number, because it is indexed every year. The trap is the myth that high pay alone makes someone a contractor. It does not. Without a valid notice in place, the whole-of-relationship test still decides whether they are really an employee, however well they are paid. If a contractor gives you a notice, file a copy with the date you received it and a record showing their earnings were over the threshold at that time. They can revoke it once. If they do, treat the relationship as exposed to the full test again and review how the work actually runs.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Opting out of the whole of relationship test","Fair Work Commission - Contractor high income threshold","Fair Work Act 2009 s15AB"}','Any engagement that looks employee-like in practice, a contractor who has revoked their notice, or any doubt about whether earnings clear the current threshold needs advice before you rely on contractor status. Getting classification wrong can create underpayment and sham contracting exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF18','federal','When someone tells you a family member has died or they have had a miscarriage, approve the leave. Under the NES, every employee, including casuals, gets 2 days'' compassionate leave each time a qualifying event happens. That covers the death or life-threatening illness or injury of an immediate family or household member, a stillbirth, or a miscarriage. Miscarriage is covered for either partner, however the pregnancy was achieved. Permanent staff are paid. Casuals take it unpaid. The trap is treating the 2 days as a yearly bank. It applies per occasion, so it can recur within weeks. The other traps are assuming casuals get nothing or that miscarriage is not covered. Both are wrong, and refusing is an NES breach. Who counts as family can be broader than you expect, so check before saying no. ''Bereavement leave'' is the everyday name for the same entitlement.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Compassionate and bereavement leave","Fair Work Ombudsman - Sick and carer''s leave and compassionate leave fact sheet","Fair Work Act 2009 ss104-106"}','Anything involving a miscarriage or stillbirth, a refusal of leave, a dispute about who counts as immediate family or household, or any performance or discipline step close in time to the leave - get advice before you act. A wrong call is an NES contravention, and action near a pregnancy loss risks a general protections or discrimination claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PF20','federal','Treat jury duty and emergency volunteering as two different things, because the pay rules differ. If a full-time or part-time employee is called for jury duty, the NES requires you to pay make-up pay for the first 10 days. That is the difference between their base rate and whatever the court pays them. Casuals can take the leave but do not get make-up pay. Emergency volunteering, like a volunteer firefighter or SES call-out, is unpaid leave any employee can take, casuals included, and there is no cap on how long it lasts. The trap is assuming all leave like this is unpaid and skipping the jury make-up pay, or refusing a call-out from a recognised emergency body. Either is an NES breach. If you have volunteers on staff, plan cover before bushfire and flood season.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Community service leave","Fair Work Ombudsman - Community service leave fact sheet","Fair Work Act 2009 ss108-112"}','Get advice if you have withheld jury duty make-up pay, refused or penalised someone for emergency service leave, or are unsure whether a volunteer group counts as a recognised emergency management body. Missed make-up pay is an underpayment, and refusing leave is an NES contravention.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('PM19','federal','Start this week by writing down who knows something nobody else does. If one person leaving tomorrow would leave you stuck, that is your key-person risk. Then pick cheap ways to spread that knowledge. Pair someone with them for job shadowing, give out stretch tasks, set up informal mentoring, and use low-cost short courses where they fill a real gap. Get critical processes written down, even roughly. A shared document beats everything living in one head. Name a likely internal successor for each key role now, not when someone resigns or you plan to exit the business. The trap is treating training as a cost and succession as a big-company thing. This is mainly a business continuity risk, not a legal one. But regulated industries can have licensing or CPD requirements, and traineeships can carry their own compliance layer under the relevant award.','{"AHRI - Quarterly Australian Work Outlook, Q3 2025 (https://www.ahri.com.au/wp-content/uploads/AHRI-WorkOutlook-Report-2025-Q3.pdf)"}','Get advice if the role needs a mandatory licence or CPD, or you are taking on a trainee or apprentice, because those carry their own compliance obligations. If succession planning means changing someone''s duties, pay or hours, or a role may no longer be needed, stop and get advice before you raise it with them.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RA9','federal','Pick two or three real reasons someone would choose your business over a bigger one, and put them in the ad in plain words. Think flexibility, autonomy, room to grow, the team, the purpose of the work, or the security of a stable local business. Test each claim: could a current employee back it up? If not, leave it out. The trap is filler like "fun, fast-paced environment", which reads as padding. The other trap is promising what you do not deliver. People who join for flexibility that never appears tend to leave early, and in a small local labour market word travels, including through online company reviews. Decide what goes in the ad and what you save for the interview. Keep every claim true, because a false claim about the job itself could raise misleading-conduct concerns under the Australian Consumer Law.','{"Department of Employment and Workplace Relations - Employee Value Proposition (https://www.dewr.gov.au/employing-and-supporting-women-your-organisation/employee-value-proposition)"}','If a candidate or new starter says the job was not what the ad promised, or you are unsure whether a claim about pay, conditions or the nature of the role is accurate, get advice before you run or repeat the ad. A materially false claim about the job could raise misleading-conduct concerns.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RA10','federal','Before you post, set a realistic time frame for replying to applicants and stick to it. Take the ad down as soon as the role is filled. To a candidate, a slow reply from a busy owner looks exactly like a ghost job, a listing with no real intent to hire. In a tight local market that reputation travels fast. There is no direct legal obligation in Australia to respond to every applicant, so the main risk is to your name rather than a claim. The exception is advertising a role you do not genuinely intend to fill. The Australian Consumer Law bans misleading conduct about employment, and that could apply. The trap is leaving ads live after you hire. Send a short acknowledgement and a short no-thanks. If volume is high, do not treat every applicant as equally serious.','{"Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (Cth) Schedule 2 (Australian Consumer Law) s31 - Misleading conduct relating to employment"}','If you are thinking about advertising a role you do not intend to fill, for pipeline-building or to look like you are growing, get advice first because it may be misleading conduct under the Australian Consumer Law. Also get advice if a candidate or regulator alleges your ad was misleading.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RA14','federal','Do a simple check this month: list everyone doing comparable work, line up what they are paid, and make sure any difference has a reason you could explain out loud. Being under 100 staff means you do not have to report your gender pay gap to WGEA. It does not mean pay equity law skips you. The Fair Work Act principle of equal remuneration for work of equal or comparable value covers employers of every size, and sex discrimination law applies regardless of size too. The trap is setting each person''s pay by one-off negotiation with no internal consistency check. That can quietly produce a gap between men and women. An affected employee, a union or the Sex Discrimination Commissioner can apply to the Fair Work Commission for an equal remuneration order. Simple pay bands for each role make pay much easier to keep fair and to explain.','{"Fair Work Ombudsman - Gender pay equity best practice guide","Workplace Gender Equality Agency - What is pay equity?","Workplace Gender Equality Agency - Publishing employer gender pay gaps","Fair Work Act 2009 Part 2-7 (Equal remuneration)","Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth)"}','If an employee, union or the Sex Discrimination Commissioner raises a pay gap, or your check finds a gap between men and women you cannot explain, get employment-law advice before changing anyone''s pay. A rushed fix can look like an admission or create a discrimination claim.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RA5','federal','Write down what the role does, and what it does not do, before you hire. Then set a point where you check again if their duties grow. In a small team everyone wears a few hats, so some flexibility is normal. The trap is that extra work piles up quietly until they raise it, or it shows up as a resignation. When duties have materially expanded, decide whether the job now sits in a higher award classification. If it does and the pay has not moved, that can be an underpayment. Check the classification against the work they actually do, not their job title, using your award or the Fair Work Pay Calculator. Be careful too about loading on duties. Expanding a role unilaterally and unreasonably can feed an unfair dismissal or constructive dismissal claim.','{"INTHEBLACK (CPA Australia) - Job creep explained and expert advice for handling it","Fair Work Ombudsman - Pay and Conditions Tool (Pay Calculator)"}','Get advice before acting if their expanded duties may now fit a higher award classification without a pay change, because that is potential underpayment. Also get advice if they object to added duties or say the job has become a different role, because that carries constructive dismissal risk.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS24','federal','Treat candidate data as regulated from first contact, and write a separate process for how you collect, use, store and dispose of it. The employee-records exemption in the Privacy Act only covers acts and practices directly related to a current employee''s employment relationship. It does not cover job applicants. The trap is thinking ''employee data is exempt anyway'' and applying that to resumes, interview notes and screening results. Those fall under the Australian Privacy Principles, unless your business sits outside the Act under the small-business exemption, so check that with the OAIC. The exemption for current staff may also narrow. Reform known as ''tranche two'' is considering narrowing or removing it, and the small-business exemption too, but this is proposed and under consultation, not law. Fix candidate handling now and keep watching the reform.','{"Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) s7B(3)","Office of the Australian Information Commissioner - Employment privacy rights"}','If you are unsure whether the Privacy Act applies to your business, have had a candidate or staff data breach, or are using AI or third-party tools to screen applicants, get privacy advice before you rely on any exemption. Mishandling candidate data creates exposure the exemption will not protect you from.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RS25','federal','Write a one-page rule for how AI is used in your hiring before you next advertise. Say which steps the tool may assist with and which decisions only a person makes, such as rejecting or shortlisting. Keep a simple record of where the tool influenced an outcome. Every few months, sample those outcomes for any group of candidates being screened out more than you would expect. Before switching on an AI feature, ask the vendor for validation evidence, how they test for bias, and how they handle candidate data. The trap is assuming the platform sorts that out. You carry the responsibility, not the vendor. Having no policy is not itself a breach. But with no record of reasonable care, a bias or privacy complaint becomes hard to defend. Decide now who handles a candidate who challenges a decision.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - I4. Lack of AI governance and vendor oversight in SME recruitment","Australian HR Institute (AHRI) - Australia needs better AI regulation, says Human Rights Commission"}','If a candidate complains that an AI-influenced decision was biased or that their data was mishandled, get advice before you respond. At that point it is a discrimination or privacy exposure, not a process tidy-up.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS10','federal','Before you test anyone, write down what the role actually needs and only use a test that measures that. If you cannot say what the test measures and why it predicts performance in this job, drop it. A generic personality test run on every role, often because the software bundled it in, adds cost and slows hiring without telling you much. The trap is treating a score as a pass or fail gate. Use results as one input alongside your own judgement, and apply the same test and threshold to every candidate for that role. Ask candidates whether they need an adjustment, because the Disability Discrimination Act obligation to make reasonable adjustments applies to assessments. An unvalidated test used as a hard cut-off is hard to defend if someone claims it unfairly screened out a group. State discrimination laws may apply too.','{"Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (Cth)","HQ.ai AU HR research report - E1. Disproportionate or irrelevant psychometric/skills testing"}','If a candidate with disability asks for an adjustment to a test, or anyone complains that a test screened them out unfairly, get advice before you respond. A mishandled adjustment request or a hard cut-off you cannot justify creates discrimination exposure.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS11','federal','Ask every candidate, at every stage, whether they need any adjustment to take part in a test or interview. Under the Disability Discrimination Act 1992, adjusting for a candidate with disability is a legal requirement, not a courtesy. That might mean extra time, a different format, or a different assessment that still measures the same job skill. The trap is ''we run the same test for everyone'': failing to adjust a process that disadvantages someone because of disability can itself be unlawful discrimination. Do not read silence as no need, because many people will not raise it unless invited. There is a limit where an adjustment would be too heavy a burden on the business, but read it narrowly, not as a way out. The same duty applies to AI-driven assessments, and state discrimination laws can also apply.','{"Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (Cth)","JobAccess - Guidelines on reasonable adjustment","JobAccess - Interviewing people with disability","AFDO - What are Reasonable Adjustments?","HQ.ai AU HR research report - E2. Reasonable adjustments in assessments for candidates with disability"}','If a candidate asks for an adjustment you think you cannot give, or you are about to reject someone after they disclosed a disability, get advice before you decide. A refusal or rejection here can be unlawful disability discrimination.',true,'review',NULL),
  ('RS15','federal','Decide now which roles get their qualifications checked directly with the issuing body, then do it every time. Licensed trades and regulated professions, such as health roles, should always be verified, not just sighted. For other roles, verify whenever the qualification is a stated requirement of the job. The trap is taking a copy or PDF at face value. Sighting the original is not verification. Organised fraud operations sell certificates claiming to be from real TAFEs and universities, and these can look convincing at a glance. Contact the institution or use a verification service. Also decide whether you check before or after making an offer, and apply that consistently. For general roles, a fake credential is mainly a fraud and negligent-hire risk. For licensed work it can also carry safety, licensing and even criminal exposure.','{"HQ.ai AU HR research report - F4. Qualification and credential verification gaps"}','If you find a fake or unverifiable qualification held by someone already working for you, get advice before you act, because any move to end their employment carries dismissal risk. If they hold a fake qualification for licensed trade or regulated work, also get advice straight away, because the safety, licensing and criminal exposure goes beyond ordinary HR.',false,'review',NULL),
  ('RS6','federal','Before you use one-way video interviews, decide whether a person or the platform''s AI makes the call. The safer setup is a human reviewing every recording, not screening people out on an AI score alone. Tell candidates up front if AI is involved, what happens to the video, how long it is kept and when it is deleted. Ask the vendor whether footage is reused to train their models. Offer another format, such as a phone or live video chat, to anyone who asks because of disability or access needs. The trap is assuming the platform carries the risk. The Privacy Act and the Australian Privacy Principles apply to the personal information you collect this way, and face and voice recordings call for extra care. New automated decision-making transparency rules will also require your privacy policy to cover decisions like an AI screen-out.','{"Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) Schedule 1 - Australian Privacy Principles","HQ.ai AU HR research report - C2. One-way AI video interviews - candidate backlash and unclear legal footing"}','If a candidate asks for an alternative format because of disability, complains about how their recording or data was used, or a screen-out rests on the AI score alone, get advice before you respond. Refusing an accessible alternative or mishandling face and voice data creates discrimination and privacy exposure.',true,'review',NULL)
